- How would you extend to 128x128 interleaving into 256 bits?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-boundary bit tricks and 128-bit reasoning without loops.\n\n## Key Concepts
- 128-bit rotation via 64-bit halves\n- Normalizing k to 0..127, masking to 128 bits\n- Pure bitwise/shifts, no branches\n\n## Code Example
```javascript
function rotl128(hi, lo, k) {
  const n = (BigInt(hi) << 64n) | BigInt(lo);
  const kk = BigInt(k) & 127n;
  const mask128 = (1n << 128n) - 1n;
  const val = ((n << kk) | (n >> (128n - kk))) & mask128;
  return {
    hi: Number((val >> 64n) & ((1n << 64n) - 1n)),
    lo: Number(val & ((1n << 64n) - 1n))
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to implement without 128-bit types?\n- How to test edge cases: k=0, k=127, k=64?\n- How to port to languages with limited integer width or without unsigned128 support?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a candidate’s ability to apply parallel bit-twiddling (SWAR) across multiple lanes in a 128-bit value, without loops, while extracting and packing results into specific lanes. It also probes attention to data layout and endianness in lane-oriented operations.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) for 32-bit lanes
- Bit masks and shifts to accumulate per-lane popcounts
- 128-bit data viewed as four 32-bit lanes and proper packing of results

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 perLanePopcount128(uint128 x) {
  auto count32 = [](uint64_t v) {
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    v = v + (v >> 8);
    v = v + (v >> 16);
    v = v + (v >> 32);
    return v & 0xFFULL;
  };

  uint64_t loCount  = count32(x.lo);
  uint64_t hiCount  = count32(x.hi);

  uint128 r = {0, 0};
  // lane0: lo low 32 bits -> low byte of r.lo
  r.lo = (loCount & 0xFFULL);
  // lane1: lo high 32 bits -> low byte of r.lo at bits 32-39
  r.lo |= (loCount & 0xFFULL) << 8;
  // lane2: hi low 32 bits -> bits 64-71 of r.hi
  r.hi = (hiCount & 0xFFULL);
  // lane3: hi high 32 bits -> bits 96-103 of r.hi
  r.hi |= (hiCount & 0xFFULL) << 8;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for arbitrary lane widths (e.g., 64-bit lanes) while preserving parallelism?
- What are the potential pitfalls with endianness or portability across compilers for the approach above?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply bit-dilation technique and Morton code basics, a common need in graphics or spatial indexing.

## Key Concepts

- Bit spreading (dilate) with masks
- Interleaving two bit streams into a 64-bit value
- No-loops approach with fixed masks

## Code Example

```c
uint64_t morton2D64(uint32_t x, uint32_t y) {
  uint64_t dx = x;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;

  uint64_t dy = y;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;

  return dx | (dy << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to 3D Morton code with x,y,z?
- What are performance considerations on 64-bit vs 32-bit platforms and compiler optimizations?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests per-byte parallel parity computation with pure bitwise ops.\n\n## Key Concepts
- 128-bit lane handling via two 64-bit halves\n- In-place parity reduction using shifts\n- Bitwise masking to extract per-byte results\n\n## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 perByteParity128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  lo ^= lo >> 4; lo ^= lo >> 2; lo ^= lo >> 1;
  hi ^= hi >> 4; hi ^= hi >> 2; hi ^= hi >> 1;
  uint64_t mask = 0x0101010101010101ULL;
  return (uint128){ .lo = lo & mask, .hi = hi & mask };
}
```
## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for non-byte-aligned lanes?\n- How would you verify correctness with minimal tests?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply known bit-hack patterns to solve a common problem without loops or libs. Shows understanding of data-parallel aggregation and portability across compilers.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) counting
- Bit masks and shifts to sum bits in chunks
- Portability across 32-bit integers

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>
uint32_t countBits32(uint32_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x55555555) + ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F);
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  x = (x & 0x0000FFFF) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF);
  return x;
}
```

