- What are alternative methods and trade-offs (lookup-table vs SWAR)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to decompose a 128‑bit value into four 32‑bit lanes and re‑assemble them without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Lane extraction via masks and shifts
- 128‑bit as hi/lo pairs with 32‑bit lanes
- Branchless permutation and safe packing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 permute32x4_128(uint128 x) {
  uint32 A = (uint32)(x.hi >> 32);
  uint32 B = (uint32)(x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  uint32 C = (uint32)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32 D = (uint32)(x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  uint128 y;
  y.hi = ((uint64_t)B << 32) | A;
  y.lo = ((uint64_t)D << 32) | C;
  return y;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to permute eight 16‑bit lanes within 128‑bit? 
- What tests validate boundary conditions (e.g., when A/B/C/D are all zeros or all ones)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of advanced bit-twiddling for permutation of set bits. Evaluates ability to craft constant-time logic without loops and to reason about edge cases of the last permutation.

## Key Concepts

- Next higher permutation with fixed popcount
- Edge-case handling (max permutation -> 0)
- Pure bitwise operations and shifts; no loops or branches
- Efficient 64-bit handling and overflow behavior

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t nextBitPermutation64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
  uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = ripple & -ripple;
  uint64_t next = ripple | (((ones ^ ripple) >> 2) / smallest);
  return next;
}
```

## Examples

- `nextBitPermutation64(0b0011)` returns `0b0101` (next permutation with 2 set bits)
- `nextBitPermutation64(0b1100)` returns `0b10000` (next permutation with 2 set bits)
- `nextBitPermutation64(0b111000)` returns `0b1000011` (next permutation with 3 set bits)

## Why This Is Asked
Interleaving bits to form Morton codes is common in spatial indexing and GPU data layouts. The solution enforces branchless bit spreading using fixed masks, ensuring deterministic performance.

## Key Concepts
- Morton order (Z-order) bit interleaving
- Fixed-mask bit spreading (part1by1)
- Branchless implementation

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline uint64_t part1by1(uint32_t n) {
  uint64_t v = n;
  v = (v | (v << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  v = (v | (v << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  v = (v | (v << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  v = (v | (v << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  v = (v | (v << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  return v;
}

uint64_t interleave32(uint32_t x, uint32_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 64-bit inputs producing a 128-bit result?
- How does this interact with SIMD paths for batched data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of bit-twiddling and performance, ensuring implementation without branching or CPU intrinsics.

## Key Concepts
- Population count (Hamming weight) via parallel reduction
- 64-bit masks and overflow-safe multiplications
- No loops or branches

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Examples
- Input: `0b10101010` (170) → Output: `4`
- Input: `0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF` → Output: `64`

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to implement a hardware-style popcount without loops or built-in functions, demonstrating understanding of bitwise manipulation techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting using bitmasks
- 32-bit unsigned arithmetic with logical shifts
- Elimination of branches and loops for performance

## Code Example
```javascript
function countBits32(n) {
  n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
  n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
  n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  n = n + (n >>> 8);
  n = n + (n >>> 16);
  return n & 0x3F;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this differ for a 64-bit implementation?
- Can you optimize this for specific input patterns?
